  "content": {
    "id": "p37-q12-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "12",
    "prompt": "Find the probability that the number of girls exceeds the number of boys in a family with:",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p37-q12-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "four children",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{5}{16}$",
          "worked": "Assume each child independently has probability $\\frac12$ of being a girl. Let $X$ count the girls. By symmetry, the two strict majorities have equal probability; exclude the tie.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(X>2)&=\\frac{1-\\Pr(X=2)}{2}\\\\\\Pr(X>2)&=\\frac{1-\\binom42(\\frac12)^4}{2}\\\\\\Pr(X>2)&=\\frac{5}{16}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p37-q12-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "five children",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac12$",
          "worked": "Assume independent births with probability $\\frac12$ of a girl. Let $X$ count the girls. A tie is impossible, and the two strict majorities have equal probability.\n$$\\Pr(X>\\tfrac52)=\\frac12.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p37-q12-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "six children",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{11}{32}$",
          "worked": "Let $X$ count the girls, with independent births and probability $\\frac12$ of a girl. Excluding the tie and using symmetry:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(X>3)&=\\frac{1-\\Pr(X=3)}{2}\\\\\\Pr(X>3)&=\\frac{1-\\binom63(\\frac12)^6}{2}\\\\\\Pr(X>3)&=\\frac{11}{32}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p37-q12-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "seven children",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac12$",
          "worked": "Assume independent births with probability $\\frac12$ of a girl. Let $X$ count the girls. A tie is impossible, and the two strict majorities have equal probability.\n$$\\Pr(X>\\tfrac72)=\\frac12.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p37-q12-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$2n$ children",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac12\\left(1-\\binom{2n}{n}\\frac{1}{2^{2n}}\\right)$\n\nExclude the tie, then halve the remaining probability by symmetry.",
          "worked": "Let $X$ count the girls, with independent births and probability $\\frac12$ of a girl. A tie occurs when $X=n$. The probabilities of a girl majority and a boy majority are equal.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(X>n)&=\\frac{1-\\Pr(X=n)}{2}\\\\\\Pr(X>n)&=\\frac{1-\\binom{2n}{n}(\\frac12)^{2n}}{2}\\\\\\Pr(X>n)&=\\frac12\\left(1-\\binom{2n}{n}\\frac{1}{2^{2n}}\\right).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      },
      {
        "id": "p37-q12-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "$2n-1$ children.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac12$\n\nAn odd family size prevents a tie, so symmetry gives equal majority probabilities.",
          "worked": "Assume independent births with probability $\\frac12$ of a girl. Let $X$ count the girls. Since $2n-1$ is odd, a tie is impossible. By symmetry, a girl majority and a boy majority are equally likely.\n$$\\Pr(X\\ge n)=\\frac12.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      }
    ]
  },
